So I wanted to do a video about it

00:00:42.879 --> 00:00:44.969
You probably remember a few years ago

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:50.399
We had the very first detection of gravitational waves which science has got very excited about and with good reason

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:55.169
everything we know about the universe so far comes from light going from

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:00.750
Radio waves through visible light through to gamma rays. That's everything we know about space

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:05.579
gravitational waves opened up a new way to look at space and they saw

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.790
Mergers between black holes and other black holes of black holes and neutron stars, and I've done a video about that detection of gravitational

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:16.679
Waves which you can see here, but the brief summary is there are two detectors in the u.s

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.809
Called LIGO and one in Italy called Virgo by combining them all together they can make

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:28.979
Detection z' and see the direction that gravitational waves come through their interferometers, which means there are an L shape

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:32.548
They send lasers and bounce them off mirrors, and then they check

00:01:33.369 --> 00:01:37.619
What happens when those lasers interfere with each other if there's a change in?

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:43.589
Dimension of one of the arms they can detect changes in size ten thousand times smaller than a proton

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.380
Which is just insane

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:50.339
also since they've been turned on they've done a bunch of

00:01:50.829 --> 00:01:55.439
Detection runs and in the last run they were seeing a gravitational wave

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:01.079
Signal on average about once every 5 days, which means that black holes are merging

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:07.380
Incredibly frequently far more frequently than we really expected before we started doing those measurements

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:11.879
So for a long time people have wanted to put a gravitational wave detector into space

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:17.519
There's good reasons for like you can make it huge. So at least it's gonna have a 2.5 million

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:24.360
Kilometer arm length, which is about six times the distance from the earth to the moon and also in space

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:30.389
It's quieter on earth ground-based detectors are susceptible to wobbling around because of seismic

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:37.080
Vibrations or a truck driving past or someone sneezing, which you don't tend to get in space

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:44.429
So that's why they wanted to send it. There trouble is space missions are obviously very expensive and prone to

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.769
Failure, so they have to make sure that it works really really really well

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:53.759
Lisa is a European Space Agency mission run in collaboration with NASA

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:59.219
Because it will be so big Lisa will be able to see things that we just can't see with the earth-based detectors

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.289
You can actually see a different frequency range

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:07.920
So Lisa will be able to see one frequency range ground-based detectors another and because they see different frequency bands

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:15.149
They're kind of a complement to each other. So Lisa will be able to see the kinds of signals that LIGO and Virgo see

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:18.869
Years and years before they actually do the final merger

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:22.440
But the final merger will actually detect with the ground-based detectors

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:29.429
the coolest thing that Lisa will be able to see is the merger of supermassive black holes because supermassive black holes are the cause of

00:03:29.829 --> 00:03:37.289
Galaxies they'll probably be surrounded by gas. So if we detect gravitational waves that look like a supermassive black hole merger

