Which we will do in the future with the Athena x-ray Observatory

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:50.519
Lisa can tell us the direction of sources to an accuracy of about a square degree

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.269
This observation would be cool because it might explain the high energy Jets

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:57.600
We see coming from the cause of certain galaxies

00:03:57.609 --> 00:04:01.829
We might even be able to see one of these Jets turn on which would be very

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:07.260
exciting and Lisa will also be able to see the absolute distance to things which is very useful to

00:04:07.389 --> 00:04:11.729
calibrate other distance measures that we use like Cepheid variables or

00:04:12.069 --> 00:04:18.929
supernovae Lisa will be able to make an independent measurement of the Hubble constant more accurately than any current technique and

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:24.119
Be able to measure it in an epoch of cosmic history where other measurements aren't available

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:29.100
The Hubble constant is a fan datian of our model of the universe and how it's expanding

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.860
So it's a very important thing to measure accurately

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:37.110
Also gravitational waves don't really get absorbed by anything as they travel through space

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:44.520
So if they're a signal strong enough Lisa will be able to see out to the very edge of the observable

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:50.759
Universe and as you're looking further away into deep space, you're also looking further back through time

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:58.770
So Lisa will allow us to study the history of how galaxies form from the early universe through to today and Lisa

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:01.320
Theoretically will be able to see

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:07.469
tiny amount of time after the Big Bang so something like a billionth of a second or even

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.650
shorter after the Big Bang and

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:15.569
We don't know what we'll see there, but it could lead to new physics

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:23.490
It could lead to us understanding how the forces of nature emerged. Maybe we'll detect some signal of new forces of nature or

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.510
Test theories that we have like cosmic super strings

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:35.520
Which is something no other current technique can do and it'll also be able to see orbiting white dwarf stars in our own galaxy

